WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.882
This phone example performs two different tasks.

00:00:02.882 --> 00:00:07.407
First, it removes non-digit characters to create a digits-only candidate.

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:11.582
Second, it checks whether the candidate has exactly ten digits.

00:00:11.582 --> 00:00:16.122
If it does not, the validated ten-digit output becomes NULL.

00:00:16.122 --> 00:00:21.117
Notice that formatting normalization does not automatically make a phone number valid.

00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:26.012
Standardization should make equivalent values comparable without inventing facts.

00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:33.412
Lowercasing an email or normalizing a known category map is different from guessing what an ambiguous value

00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:37.973
was supposed to mean. Good cleaning reduces representation noise while preserving uncertainty.
